///1ZSID
Help by Willem Aandewiel
ZSID - Symbolic debugger for the Z80

///2STARTING
  E>A:ZSID DEMO.COM
  E>A:ZSID DEMO.COM DEMO.SYM

The second example loads the matching symbols as well.
Use the SYM file from the same program build.
ZSID requires a Z80. DDT is the usual 8080 debugger.

///2EXAMINING
D displays memory, L disassembles code and X shows processor status.
Addresses are normally hexadecimal.
Examples at the debugger prompt:

  D100,13F
  L100
  X

///2EXECUTION
T steps through instructions while displaying the processor state.
G starts or resumes execution; G100 starts at 0100H.
G0 returns to CP/M.
The debugger really executes program code. It is not a simulation
isolated from files and devices.

///2SYMBOLS
ZSID can use loaded symbol names when examining a program.
Additional tracing and histogram features may need separate
utilities.
This page describes the basics of Digital Research ZSID.
